#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087390 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "739a8f10-413a-5a1d-a47e-4afd0b14eb44" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Homogeneous and Inhomogeneous ODEs"}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "6f249bfa-e57b-5784-9da8-d0f9aff213bc" :question/math-academy-id "e-21813" :question/problem "Which of the following first-order linear differential equations are inhomogeneous?\n\n- $y' + x^3 y = 0$\n\n- $y' + x^3 y = x^4$\n\n- $y' + x^3 y = y$" :question/requires-calculator false :question/worked-solution "A first-order linear differential equation is homogeneous if it can be written in the form\n\n$$\ny' + P(x)y = 0.\n$$\n\nA first-order linear differential equation is inhomogeneous if it is not homogeneous, i.e., if it can be written as\n\n$$\ny' + P(x)y = Q(x)\n$$\n\nwhere $Q$ is not identical to zero.\n\nTo determine which equations are inhomogeneous, we examine each one in turn.\n\n- First, we consider the equation\n\n$$\ny' + x^3 y = 0.\n$$\n\nNotice that this has the form $y' + P(x)y = 0.$ Therefore, this equation is homogeneous.\n\n- Next, we consider the equation\n\n$$\ny' + x^3 y = x^4.\n$$\n\nNotice that this is written in the form $y' + P(x)y = Q(x)$ where $Q(x) = x^4$ is not identically zero. Therefore, this equation is inhomogeneous.\n\n- Finally, we consider the equation\n\n$$\ny' + x^3 y = y.\n$$\n\nWe collect the $y$-terms on the left side:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′}+{x}^{3}y & =y \\\\ {y}^{′}+{x}^{3}y-y & =0 \\\\ {y}^{′}+({x}^{3}-1)y & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince our equation can be written in the form $y' + P(x)y = 0,$ the equation is homogeneous.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087538 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "7de671ee-7bba-54ce-bec2-d2d6f5569f4a" :knowledge-point/title "Writing First-Order Linear ODEs in Standard Form"}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "dc93ac2d-3588-5dc4-9cf8-3df1f17c353d" :question/math-academy-id "e-6599" :question/problem "Write the following first-order linear ODE in standard form.\n\n$$\n\\dfrac {\\textrm{d}y} {\\textrm{d}x}\n+\ny\\sin x\n-\n\\cos x\n=0\n$$" :question/requires-calculator false :question/worked-solution "A first-order linear ODE is in standard form if it is written as\n\n$$\n\\dfrac {\\textrm{d}y} {\\textrm{d}x}\n+\nP(x)y\n=\nQ(x).\n$$\n\nConverting the given equation to standard form, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+y\\operatorname{sin}⁡x-\\operatorname{cos}⁡x & =0 \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+y\\operatorname{sin}⁡x & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡x \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+{{(\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)}_{⏟}}_{P(x)}y & ={{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}_{⏟}}_{Q(x)}\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, the equation is written in standard form with $P(x)=\\sin x$ and $Q(x) = \\cos x.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087539 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "bc50a680-dbf8-5d45-a8c3-72c3c15243f6" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying First-Order Linear ODEs"}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "2a6d7a3a-38a4-5925-8d1a-3d85b74ad350" :question/math-academy-id "e-6600" :question/problem "Determine whether the following equation is a first-order linear ODE:\n\n$$\nx^2\\dfrac {\\textrm{d}y} {\\textrm{d}x}\n+\n\\sin(2x)y\n=\ne^x.\n$$" :question/requires-calculator false :question/worked-solution "We attempt to write the equation in the standard form\n\n$$\n\\dfrac {\\textrm{d}y} {\\textrm{d}x}\n+\nP(x)y\n=\nQ(x) \\,.\n$$\n\nDividing the equation through by $x^2$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)y & ={e}^{x} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)}{{x}^{2}}y & =\\frac{{e}^{x}}{{x}^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can see that this equation is now in standard form with $P(x) = \\dfrac{\\sin{2x}}{x^2}$ and $Q(x) = \\dfrac{e^x}{x^2}.$\n\nBecause the equation can be written in standard form, we conclude that it is a first-order linear ODE."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186295528 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "7de671ee-7bba-54ce-bec2-d2d6f5569f4a" :knowledge-point/title "Writing First-Order Linear ODEs in Standard Form"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "14ab8be4-03ca-5153-8c0c-7088d7ffafdb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+2y={e}^{-2x}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295530} {:answer/id #uuid "17484bfe-f934-5d3c-9c77-b9ea78107716" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}-y={e}^{-2x}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295531} {:answer/id #uuid "56a46fe8-9518-53fc-bc8b-5e33057c268a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+2{e}^{-x}y-{e}^{-2x}x=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295532} {:answer/id #uuid "d284da86-d9f8-5315-b5db-976f864dde0d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+2{e}^{-x}y={e}^{-2x}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295533} {:answer/id #uuid "a45c7eaf-12b3-56fe-82d4-fd5314e7b4c6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+{e}^{-2x}y-{e}^{-x}x=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295534}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d284da86-d9f8-5315-b5db-976f864dde0d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+2{e}^{-x}y={e}^{-2x}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295533} :answer-field/id #uuid "0894869c-bc84-5110-ae10-0563263bcd72" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295529}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "6070c390-c527-5587-a620-4ee028211b12" :question/math-academy-id "q-103349" :question/problem "Write the following first-order linear ODE in standard form.\n\n$$\n{e}^{2x}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+2{e}^{x}y-x=0\n$$" :question/worked-solution "A first-order linear ODE is in standard form if it is written as\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+P(x)y=Q(x).\n$$\n\nConverting the given equation to standard form, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{e}^{2x}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+2{e}^{x}y-x & =0 \\\\ {e}^{2x}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+2{e}^{x}y & =x \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+{{(2{e}^{-x})}_{⏟}}_{P(x)}y & ={{{e}^{-2x}x}_{⏟}}_{Q(x)}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, the equation is written in standard form with $P(x)=2{e}^{-x}$ and $Q(x)={e}^{-2x}x.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186295535 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "7de671ee-7bba-54ce-bec2-d2d6f5569f4a" :knowledge-point/title "Writing First-Order Linear ODEs in Standard Form"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "cf6a7f40-b2a6-5fd4-9b0f-2e5200133a8e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}-(\\operatorname{cot}⁡x)y=2\\operatorname{cos}⁡x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295537} {:answer/id #uuid "a7a5b571-7dc4-55fc-95b4-794b2cf751c5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+(\\operatorname{sec}⁡x)y=-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295538} {:answer/id #uuid "b73c2a1a-ae93-5d95-a7f2-5a426699abed" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}-(\\operatorname{tan}⁡x)y=-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295539} {:answer/id #uuid "2269ae32-16f1-57f5-940e-8b5cbf1d092a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x=(\\operatorname{sec}⁡x)y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295540} {:answer/id #uuid "982f6313-1aff-5013-864c-7f870615840d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+2\\operatorname{sin}⁡x=(\\operatorname{tan}⁡x)y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295541}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b73c2a1a-ae93-5d95-a7f2-5a426699abed" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}-(\\operatorname{tan}⁡x)y=-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295539} :answer-field/id #uuid "7642a827-a0ad-568b-ba36-f985cbc48b0f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295536}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "1de67dfe-a866-57fb-b3b3-9dacfc2c3c42" :question/math-academy-id "q-103354" :question/problem "Write the following first-order linear ODE in standard form.\n\n$$\n(\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x-(\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)\\,y=0\n$$" :question/worked-solution "A first-order linear ODE is in standard form if it is written as\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+P(x)y=Q(x).\n$$\n\nConverting the given equation to standard form, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x-(\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)\\,y & =0 \\\\ (\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}-(\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)\\,y & =-\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}-(\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x})y & =-\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}-(\\operatorname{tan}⁡x)y & =-\\frac{2\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+{{(-\\operatorname{tan}⁡x)}_{⏟}}_{P(x)}y & ={{-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}_{⏟}}_{Q(x)}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, the equation is written in standard form with $P(x)=-\\operatorname{tan}⁡x$ and $Q(x)=-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡x.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186295542 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "bc50a680-dbf8-5d45-a8c3-72c3c15243f6" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying First-Order Linear ODEs"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3f225529-def3-54e2-bfe2-a307d38b0b28" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}-\\frac{{e}^{x}}{x}y={x}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295544} {:answer/id #uuid "58bd1127-2f80-596c-9cc9-160f2ff5de85" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x})}^{2}-{x}^{3}=\\sqrt{1+y}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295545} {:answer/id #uuid "f7f51840-b743-5f38-a266-57c6a624f8c0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{{y}^{2}}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}-{x}^{2}=\\operatorname{sin}⁡x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295546} {:answer/id #uuid "de36a199-47ef-5bf5-b198-80d287b3edb6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}-{e}^{xy}=x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295547} {:answer/id #uuid "fff47300-ef22-525e-a49b-c6e3906d3c77" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+x\\operatorname{ln}⁡(xy)={x}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295548}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "3f225529-def3-54e2-bfe2-a307d38b0b28" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}-\\frac{{e}^{x}}{x}y={x}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295544} :answer-field/id #uuid "75b6b86d-4093-52ff-a948-ad44ddaf7ebd" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295543}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "a3a7bdfb-b358-530e-a735-49fe9ab8c790" :question/math-academy-id "q-103554" :question/problem "Which of the following is a first-order linear ordinary differential equation?" :question/worked-solution "To determine which of the following differential equations is a first-order linear ordinary differential equation, we attempt to write each option in the standard form.\n\nA first-order linear ODE is in standard form if it is written as\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+P(x)y=Q(x).\n$$\n\nNote that the option\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}-\\frac{{e}^{x}}{x}y={x}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\n$$\n\nis already is written in standard form with  $P(x)=-\\frac{{e}^{x}}{x}$ and $Q(x)={x}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x.$ Since the equation is written in standard form, we conclude that it is a first-order linear ODE.\n\nAll of the other equations cannot be written in the standard form, and therefore they are not first-order linear ODEs."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186295549 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "bc50a680-dbf8-5d45-a8c3-72c3c15243f6" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying First-Order Linear ODEs"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "7584b616-af6d-582e-9b8d-c0bb967a0d21" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}-({x}^{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)y=\\operatorname{sin}⁡x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295551} {:answer/id #uuid "88ae4463-08e0-520a-b673-02814dfe9ee3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}-\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}y={e}^{-x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295552} {:answer/id #uuid "19164194-3ecb-5723-b5d1-ec2d3c2a4a38" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{x}^{2}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}-(1+x)y=1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295553} {:answer/id #uuid "e5f134dd-e5d7-5f65-92d8-834db9e651d5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}-\\sqrt{xy}={x}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295554} {:answer/id #uuid "54d24165-de9b-5308-806d-9579fb252e4c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}-4y=x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295555}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e5f134dd-e5d7-5f65-92d8-834db9e651d5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}-\\sqrt{xy}={x}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295554} :answer-field/id #uuid "3ab4010a-68ac-5779-a080-d97ea3f4d432" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295550}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "2e2df44b-885f-5606-befe-9f70e9e645a5" :question/math-academy-id "q-103557" :question/problem "Which of the following is **NOT** a first-order linear ordinary differential equation?" :question/worked-solution "To determine which of the following differential equations is a first-order linear ordinary differential equation, we attempt to write each option in the standard form.\n\nA first-order linear ODE is in standard form if it is written as\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+P(x)y=Q(x).\n$$\n\nNote that the option\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}-\\sqrt{xy}={x}^{2}\n$$\n\ncannot be written in standard form since the power of $y$ in the term $\\sqrt{xy}$ is not $1.$ Since the equation cannot be written in standard form, we conclude that it is not a first-order linear ODE.\n\nAll of the other equations can be written in the standard form, and therefore they are first-order linear ODEs."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186295556 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "bc50a680-dbf8-5d45-a8c3-72c3c15243f6" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying First-Order Linear ODEs"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0f87f4c9-db69-5310-b5e7-ecd975161900" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+\\operatorname{ln}⁡(xy)={x}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295558} {:answer/id #uuid "634dec06-3b47-5c24-8b2d-3ef78d6de87f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+4y={e}^{x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295559} {:answer/id #uuid "cea1dc7a-0c4b-575b-aa74-2dd609fb1b9c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}-xy=\\operatorname{sin}⁡x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295560} {:answer/id #uuid "dbe214a8-c8ac-5d10-b793-cc97e74c1bf9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{x}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}-{x}^{2}y=\\operatorname{sin}⁡x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295561} {:answer/id #uuid "ae0d096a-2522-5ff0-a17d-ed868c61e17f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+(\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)y={x}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295562}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0f87f4c9-db69-5310-b5e7-ecd975161900" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+\\operatorname{ln}⁡(xy)={x}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295558} :answer-field/id #uuid "a8367e86-2a93-5802-b40f-6ccdd04f329b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295557}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "4f6f3d9f-1dcd-5dfc-ad09-04a9f798bb93" :question/math-academy-id "q-103559" :question/problem "Which of the following is **NOT** a first-order linear ordinary differential equation?" :question/worked-solution "To determine which of the following differential equations is a first-order linear ordinary differential equation, we attempt to write each option in the standard form.\n\nA first-order linear ODE is in standard form if it is written as\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+P(x)y=Q(x).\n$$\n\nNote that the option\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+\\operatorname{ln}⁡(xy)={x}^{2}\n$$\n\ncannot be written in standard form since the power of $y$ in the term $\\operatorname{ln}⁡(xy)$ is not $1.$ Since the equation cannot be written in standard form, we conclude that it is not a first-order linear ODE.\n\nAll of the other equations can be written in the standard form, and therefore they are first-order linear ODEs."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186295563 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "7de671ee-7bba-54ce-bec2-d2d6f5569f4a" :knowledge-point/title "Writing First-Order Linear ODEs in Standard Form"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "f1b3e5f7-7eb7-5511-81b1-23c5b6aea1a8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{dy}{dx}+y\\,{(\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)}^{2}={(1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295565} {:answer/id #uuid "0ed56dab-049e-5c4b-ba10-c8a03243f80d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{dy}{dx}=1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295566} {:answer/id #uuid "6a4a410c-9ad1-5027-bb91-c23197893d52" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{dy}{dx}+y\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡x=1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295567} {:answer/id #uuid "a01982d2-9f85-569c-8517-2a0d4aed07ac" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{dy}{dx}+2y\\operatorname{cos}⁡x=1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295568} {:answer/id #uuid "42d23912-7fe2-56fc-8591-337db7f05398" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{dy}{dx}+4y\\operatorname{cos}⁡x=1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295569}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a01982d2-9f85-569c-8517-2a0d4aed07ac" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{dy}{dx}+2y\\operatorname{cos}⁡x=1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295568} :answer-field/id #uuid "76a95351-2164-5d4a-84eb-f7670061af50" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295564}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "aa9b6eb5-384a-59f1-95c3-f2beb341858b" :question/math-academy-id "q-16401" :question/problem "Write the following first-order linear ODE in standard form.\n\n$$\n\\frac{dy}{dx}+{y}^{2}{(\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)}^{2}={(1-y\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)}^{2}\\,.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "A first-order linear ODE is in standard form if it is written as\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+P(x)y=Q(x).\n$$\n\nConverting the given equation to standard form, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{dy}{dx}+{y}^{2}{(\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)}^{2} & ={(1-y\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)}^{2} \\\\ \\frac{dy}{dx}+{y}^{2}{(\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)}^{2} & =1+{y}^{2}{(\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)}^{2}-2y\\operatorname{cos}⁡x \\\\ \\frac{dy}{dx} & =1-2y\\operatorname{cos}⁡x \\\\ \\frac{dy}{dx}+{{2\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}_{⏟}}_{P(x)}\\,y & ={{1}_{⏟}}_{Q(x)}\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, the equation is written in standard form with $P(x)=2\\operatorname{cos}⁡x$ and $Q(x)=1.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186295570 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "739a8f10-413a-5a1d-a47e-4afd0b14eb44" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Homogeneous and Inhomogeneous ODEs"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1219c8ce-b99f-51b9-93cb-8aa2c264a5c7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295572} {:answer/id #uuid "af3d3b41-1abc-5add-b1ee-6fb3df674df2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295573} {:answer/id #uuid "d93abfba-138d-5d5c-b1b7-c518978566ad" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295574} {:answer/id #uuid "5f4fbc36-6177-5379-b397-43d14ad220a1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295575} {:answer/id #uuid "e3b9d07b-dd52-5690-9c5a-a6e17e8a5071" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295576}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "af3d3b41-1abc-5add-b1ee-6fb3df674df2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295573} :answer-field/id #uuid "4580721f-257e-550b-859d-b876e3e3adc3" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295571}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "ba5d1a07-b366-5225-a5ad-9036dc55130b" :question/math-academy-id "q-331605" :question/problem "Which of the following first-order linear differential equations are inhomogeneous?\n\n- ${y}^{′}+{x}^{2}y=3x$\n- ${y}^{′}+{x}^{2}y=0$\n- ${y}^{′}+{x}^{2}y=y$" :question/worked-solution "A first-order linear differential equation is homogeneous if it can be written in the form\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}+P(x)y=0.\n$$\n\nA first-order linear differential equation is inhomogeneous if it is not homogeneous, i.e., if it can be written as\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}+P(x)y=Q(x)\n$$\n\nwhere $Q$ is not identical to zero.\n\nTo determine which equations are inhomogeneous, we examine each one in turn.\n\n- First, we consider the equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}+{x}^{2}y=3x.\n$$\n\nNotice that this is written in the form ${y}^{′}+P(x)y=Q(x)$ where $Q(x)=3x$ is not identically zero. Therefore, this equation is inhomogeneous.\n\n- Next, we consider the equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}+{x}^{2}y=0.\n$$\n\nNotice that this equation has the form ${y}^{′}+P(x)y=0.$ Therefore, this equation is homogeneous.\n\n- Finally, we consider the equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}+{x}^{2}y=y.\n$$\n\nWe collect the $y$-terms on the left side:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′}+{x}^{2}y & =y \\\\ {y}^{′}+{x}^{2}y-y & =0 \\\\ {y}^{′}+({x}^{2}-1)y & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince our equation can be written in the form ${y}^{′}+P(x)y=0,$ the equation is homogeneous.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186295577 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "739a8f10-413a-5a1d-a47e-4afd0b14eb44" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Homogeneous and Inhomogeneous ODEs"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8926c775-f708-509f-b729-ae9b0f58a55c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295579} {:answer/id #uuid "0c2d0491-3e44-5f57-b48b-6bf0266e9169" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295580} {:answer/id #uuid "8faa1cb6-9fd9-5deb-8970-f47ead1940de" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295581} {:answer/id #uuid "56872001-7dfa-5f60-b17a-51ac8b5340eb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295582} {:answer/id #uuid "41bf1c9b-5969-5893-a763-950253bf81e8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295583}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8926c775-f708-509f-b729-ae9b0f58a55c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295579} :answer-field/id #uuid "7476eee5-cb0b-5334-be50-5331657389fc" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295578}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "aa88aa57-e351-5997-9157-8dc2cdacbeb3" :question/math-academy-id "q-331725" :question/problem "Which of the following first-order linear differential equations are homogeneous?\n\n- ${y}^{′}-xy=-y$\n- ${y}^{′}-xy=0$\n- ${y}^{′}-xy=-5y$" :question/worked-solution "A first-order linear differential equation is homogeneous if it can be written in the form\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}+P(x)y=0.\n$$\n\nNow, let's consider each equation in turn.\n\n- First, we consider the equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}-xy=-y.\n$$\n\nWe move the term involving $y$ to the left side so that all $y$-terms are together:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′}-xy & =-y \\\\ {y}^{′}-xy+y & =0 \\\\ {y}^{′}-(x-1)y & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis matches the homogeneous form ${y}^{′}+P(x)y=0.$  Therefore, this equation *is* homogeneous.\n\n- Next, we consider the equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}-xy=0.\n$$\n\nThe right-hand side is already zero, so the equation directly matches the form ${y}^{′}+P(x)y=0.$ Therefore, this equation is homogeneous.\n\n- Finally, we consider the equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}-xy=-5y.\n$$\n\nWe move the term involving $y$ to the left side so that all $y$-terms are together:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′}-xy & =-5y \\\\ {y}^{′}-xy+5y & =0 \\\\ {y}^{′}-(x-5)y & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis matches the homogeneous form ${y}^{′}+P(x)y=0.$  Therefore, this equation *is* homogeneous.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, II and III.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186295584 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "739a8f10-413a-5a1d-a47e-4afd0b14eb44" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Homogeneous and Inhomogeneous ODEs"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b1d69bcd-a815-59a5-a930-0421b92726c5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295586} {:answer/id #uuid "834b6985-f57b-5121-bb1a-e3fa7fd0126e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295587} {:answer/id #uuid "2fefd4a5-92f8-53cc-aa51-7e5b947e9e36" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295588} {:answer/id #uuid "1d7bbc1a-9ef5-5007-a30f-7c3936fc8ae2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295589} {:answer/id #uuid "ea30125f-7d05-5cc2-a6c5-867b0341624e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295590}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1d7bbc1a-9ef5-5007-a30f-7c3936fc8ae2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295589} :answer-field/id #uuid "b45c29b5-3a19-58b4-b7ee-08c071cee876" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295585}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "0b3d994f-127d-584d-941d-d7165ce6ea2e" :question/math-academy-id "q-331728" :question/problem "Which of the following first-order linear differential equations are homogeneous?\n\n- ${y}^{′}-4xy=y$\n- ${y}^{′}-4xy=0$\n- ${y}^{′}-4xy=9$" :question/worked-solution "A first-order linear differential equation is homogeneous if it can be written in the form\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}+P(x)y=0.\n$$\n\nNow, let's consider each equation in turn.\n\n- First, we consider the equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}-4xy=y.\n$$\n\nWe move the term involving $y$ to the left side so that all $y$-terms are together:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′}-4xy & =y \\\\ {y}^{′}-4xy-y & =0 \\\\ {y}^{′}-(4x+1)y & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis matches the homogeneous form ${y}^{′}+P(x)y=0.$ Therefore, this equation is homogeneous.\n\n- Next, we consider the equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}-4xy=0.\n$$\n\nThe right-hand side is already zero, so the equation directly matches the form ${y}^{′}+P(x)y=0.$ Therefore, this equation is homogeneous.\n\n- Finally, we consider the equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}-4xy=9.\n$$\n\nThe presence of the constant term $9$ means the right-hand side is not zero, and so it cannot be rewritten in homogeneous form. Thus, this equation is *not* homogeneous.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186295591 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "739a8f10-413a-5a1d-a47e-4afd0b14eb44" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Homogeneous and Inhomogeneous ODEs"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5cf227c3-f20d-5d7f-9976-825b249bfda5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295593} {:answer/id #uuid "9f554a0b-7bff-54aa-bab2-e475db2cf532" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295594} {:answer/id #uuid "d5d29575-c29b-56fe-af62-9f3fab6e0818" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295595} {:answer/id #uuid "28dc1cdf-dce1-503b-b046-0ff022affc17" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295596} {:answer/id #uuid "b27b7a48-7b2f-5251-afb0-8fef0d46a904" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295597}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9f554a0b-7bff-54aa-bab2-e475db2cf532" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295594} :answer-field/id #uuid "498d53ab-9a70-5608-b2fc-0ccb36cb644b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295592}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "c2e6adcc-e613-5599-bfa6-8b950fbde93e" :question/math-academy-id "q-331738" :question/problem "Which of the following first-order linear differential equations are inhomogeneous?\n\n- ${y}^{′}+4xy=2$\n- ${y}^{′}+4xy=0$\n- ${y}^{′}+4xy=y$" :question/worked-solution "A first-order linear differential equation is homogeneous if it can be written in the form\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}+P(x)y=0.\n$$\n\nA first-order linear differential equation is inhomogeneous if it is not homogeneous, i.e., if it can be written as\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}+P(x)y=Q(x)\n$$\n\nwhere $Q$ is not identical to zero.\n\nTo determine which equations are inhomogeneous, we examine each one in turn.\n\n- First, we consider the equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}+4xy=2.\n$$\n\nNotice that this is written in the form ${y}^{′}+P(x)y=Q(x)$ where $Q(x)=2$ is not identically zero. Therefore, this equation is inhomogeneous.\n\n- Next, we consider the equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}+4xy=0.\n$$\n\nThe right-hand side is already zero, so the equation directly matches the form ${y}^{′}+P(x)y=0.$ Therefore, this equation is homogeneous.\n\n- Finally, we consider the equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}+4xy=y.\n$$\n\nWe collect the $y$-terms on the left side:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′}+4xy & =y \\\\ {y}^{′}+4xy-y & =0 \\\\ {y}^{′}+(4x-1)y & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis matches the homogeneous form ${y}^{′}+P(x)y=0.$ Therefore, this equation is homogeneous.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186295598 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "739a8f10-413a-5a1d-a47e-4afd0b14eb44" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Homogeneous and Inhomogeneous ODEs"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "17e83b02-98ac-5154-991f-ea324e542e0a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295600} {:answer/id #uuid "2b347fce-b481-5202-b2fc-93db6df2d72d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295601} {:answer/id #uuid "6c825e5a-ab81-5ea1-bc36-515c2d3782cb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295602} {:answer/id #uuid "b166383d-e2ac-5fd0-94d3-505aed1d2c73" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295603} {:answer/id #uuid "6bdd567a-a096-58c1-af62-7cd2d359860f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295604}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "6bdd567a-a096-58c1-af62-7cd2d359860f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295604} :answer-field/id #uuid "8b0f402c-7608-59dd-aeba-5a373c2dd659" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295599}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "eae27715-507a-55b6-be9e-f90384a5f6f9" :question/math-academy-id "q-331742" :question/problem "Which of the following first-order linear differential equations are inhomogeneous?\n\n- ${y}^{′}+2y=y$\n- ${y}^{′}+2y=x$\n- ${y}^{′}+2y=0$" :question/worked-solution "A first-order linear differential equation is homogeneous if it can be written in the form\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}+P(x)y=0.\n$$\n\nA first-order linear differential equation is inhomogeneous if it is not homogeneous, i.e., if it can be written as\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}+P(x)y=Q(x)\n$$\n\nwhere $Q$ is not identical to zero.\n\nTo determine which equations are inhomogeneous, we examine each one in turn.\n\n- First, we consider the equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}+2y=y.\n$$\n\nWe collect the $y$-terms on the left side:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′}+2y & =y \\\\ {y}^{′}+2y-y & =0 \\\\ {y}^{′}+y & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis matches the homogeneous form ${y}^{′}+P(x)y=0.$  Therefore, this equation *is* homogeneous.\n\n- Next, we consider the equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}+2y=x.\n$$\n\nNotice that this is written in the form ${y}^{′}+P(x)y=Q(x)$ where $Q(x)=x$ is not identically zero. Therefore, this equation is inhomogeneous.\n\n- Finally, we consider the equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}+2y=0.\n$$\n\nThis matches the homogeneous form ${y}^{′}+P(x)y=0.$  Therefore, this equation *is* homogeneous.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186295605 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "bc50a680-dbf8-5d45-a8c3-72c3c15243f6" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying First-Order Linear ODEs"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "eae848a5-d051-5403-a7bd-4503d2aeec25" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+x{y}^{2}=2y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295607} {:answer/id #uuid "40a4fe6d-788f-520e-95f5-295427c35a7f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+y=1+\\operatorname{sin}⁡x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295608} {:answer/id #uuid "ed29cfd2-104b-538a-9ddc-978e4127d551" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+\\frac{y}{2}=x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295609} {:answer/id #uuid "b82dcafa-ff41-54c0-9644-1d4421403a4c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+{x}^{2}y=\\operatorname{sec}⁡x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295610} {:answer/id #uuid "aab46c36-3f8d-5731-84e3-381f12536131" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+xy={y}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295611}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "aab46c36-3f8d-5731-84e3-381f12536131" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+xy={y}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295611} :answer-field/id #uuid "b040869a-142e-599b-b4c0-97b901641032" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295606}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "b90097c6-1885-5995-97db-70461131bc55" :question/math-academy-id "q-79750" :question/problem "Which of the following is **NOT** a first-order linear ordinary differential equation?" :question/worked-solution "To determine which of the following differential equations is a first-order linear ordinary differential equation, we attempt to write each option in the standard form.\n\nA first-order linear ODE is in standard form if it is written as\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+P(x)y=Q(x).\n$$\n\nNote that the option\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+xy={y}^{2}\n$$\n\ncannot be written in standard form since the right-hand side depends on $y.$ Since the equation cannot be written in standard form, we conclude that it is not a first-order linear ODE.\n\nAll of the other equations can be written in the standard form, and therefore they are first-order linear ODEs."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186295612 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "bc50a680-dbf8-5d45-a8c3-72c3c15243f6" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying First-Order Linear ODEs"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "002576cf-2ed9-5b4b-85f5-930244d59605" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{{y}^{2}+3x}{y}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295614} {:answer/id #uuid "0d0692bd-bd42-52cf-a2ae-91371e3aa073" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(\\frac{1}{y})\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{y-x}{{y}^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295615} {:answer/id #uuid "5b7b52c0-c9b5-553d-82dc-5e67fbc14823" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}={y}^{2}-2x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295616} {:answer/id #uuid "6da0902d-c64e-5abc-b9d3-c24e178f8591" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(\\frac{1}{y})\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{2y+{x}^{2}}{y}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295617} {:answer/id #uuid "d2a1ecc5-ec11-54dc-a466-7ca7e0f11cdc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(\\frac{1}{y})\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{1}{y\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}-y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295618}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "6da0902d-c64e-5abc-b9d3-c24e178f8591" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(\\frac{1}{y})\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{2y+{x}^{2}}{y}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295617} :answer-field/id #uuid "10b53c59-d11e-5e30-896d-6442479255cf" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295613}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "438bd143-91da-5d1a-8869-3ed754e59004" :question/math-academy-id "q-79754" :question/problem "Which of the following is a first-order linear ordinary differential equation?" :question/worked-solution "To determine which of the following differential equations is a first-order linear ordinary differential equation, we attempt to write each option in the standard form.\n\nA first-order linear ODE is in standard form if it is written as\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+P(x)y=Q(x).\n$$\n\nLet's attempt to write $(\\frac{1}{y})\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{2y+{x}^{2}}{y}$ in the standard form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(\\frac{1}{y})\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{2y+{x}^{2}}{y} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =y(\\frac{2y+{x}^{2}}{y}) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =2y+{x}^{2} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}-2y & ={x}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis differential equation is in standard form with  $P(x)=-2$ and $Q(x)={x}^{2}.$ Since the equation is written in standard form, we conclude that it is a first-order linear ODE.\n\nAll of the other equations cannot be written in the standard form, and therefore they are not first-order linear ODEs."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088364 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "7de671ee-7bba-54ce-bec2-d2d6f5569f4a" :knowledge-point/title "Writing First-Order Linear ODEs in Standard Form"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a0e9f778-66fa-54e7-acc6-001b43aaaaa0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{dy}{dx} + \\frac{1}{x}y = 2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186087115} {:answer/id #uuid "c28a08c9-ed5f-57c9-a678-ebf32e926c84" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x\\frac{dy}{dx} + y = 2x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186087116} {:answer/id #uuid "f09d6fcb-7508-53dc-9ebf-082a30a5867b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{dy}{dx} + \\frac{1}{x}y - 2 = 0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186087117} {:answer/id #uuid "1046320f-9c93-59e8-ae7e-8dbe81821495" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{dy}{dx} =-\\frac{1}{x}y + 2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186087118} {:answer/id #uuid "4bc89d23-a930-5ede-b341-6ac4428f6277" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y = 2x - x\\frac{dy}{dx}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186087119}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a0e9f778-66fa-54e7-acc6-001b43aaaaa0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{dy}{dx} + \\frac{1}{x}y = 2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186087115} :answer-field/id #uuid "46b999b3-f12d-5d5e-aa21-cfda0ea51796" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186087114}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "9fbd67f9-88e1-5554-8625-84ac1e940645" :question/math-academy-id "q-8359" :question/problem "Write the following first-order linear ODE in standard form.\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{2x-y}{x}\\,.\n$$" :question/requires-calculator false :question/worked-solution "A first-order linear ODE is in standard form if it is written as\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+P(x)y=Q(x).\n$$\n\nConverting the given equation to standard form, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{2x-y}{x} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{2x}{x}-\\frac{y}{x} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & +\\frac{y}{x}=\\frac{2x}{x} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & +{{\\frac{1}{x}}_{⏟}}_{P(x)}y={{2}_{⏟}}_{Q(x)}\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, the equation is written in standard form with $P(x)=\\frac{1}{x}$ and $Q(x)=2.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186295619 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "7de671ee-7bba-54ce-bec2-d2d6f5569f4a" :knowledge-point/title "Writing First-Order Linear ODEs in Standard Form"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "cd231e8b-fddb-5596-b172-1d4684b3ce8b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x=-2y\\operatorname{cot}⁡x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295621} {:answer/id #uuid "61c9df9f-9b53-54a0-a50c-939edf359046" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=-2y\\operatorname{cot}⁡x-\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295622} {:answer/id #uuid "67f112c2-6aea-51fd-9998-90163bc7eeef" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2y\\operatorname{cot}⁡x=-\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}-\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295623} {:answer/id #uuid "3ceb329e-55a9-5c86-8bfe-ee20fd3b57a5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=-\\frac{1}{2\\operatorname{cot}⁡x}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}-\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x}{2\\operatorname{cot}⁡x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295624} {:answer/id #uuid "dfa73437-0d7e-5210-9b89-3e08b4dc41c4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+2(\\operatorname{cot}⁡x)y=-\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295625}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "dfa73437-0d7e-5210-9b89-3e08b4dc41c4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+2(\\operatorname{cot}⁡x)y=-\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295625} :answer-field/id #uuid "2f9f48bd-0e67-5e30-8478-edffa5bce95f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295620}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "ef24453f-f045-5d0a-8ed3-0ca5b930bc74" :question/math-academy-id "q-8360" :question/problem "Write the following first-order linear ODE in standard form.\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+2y\\operatorname{cot}⁡x+\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x=0\\,.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "A first-order linear ODE is in standard form if it is written as\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+P(x)y=Q(x).\n$$\n\nConverting the given equation to standard form, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+2y\\operatorname{cot}⁡x+\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x & =0 \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+2y\\operatorname{cot}⁡x & =-\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+{{2(\\operatorname{cot}⁡x)}_{⏟}}_{P(x)}y & ={{-\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x}_{⏟}}_{Q(x)}\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, the equation is written in standard form with $P(x)=2\\operatorname{cot}⁡x$ and $Q(x)=-\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x.$"}]}
